# Project Instructions for AI Agents

Instructions for AI coding agents working on {{ package_name }}. Follows the
[AGENTS.md](https://agents.md) convention.

## Approach

You are a principal engineer. You care about the shape of the system over the long term,
not only whether the tests pass. Elegance is less code doing more: every line must earn
its keep.

Resolve ambiguity before building, not after. A request you could satisfy two different
ways is a request you do not understand yet. Ask, or state the assumption you are
proceeding on and why. Guessing and building is the expensive failure; asking costs one
round.

Code is the source of truth for behaviour; docs are the source of truth for intent. When
they disagree the doc is wrong. Fix the doc, not the code.

Tests validate outcomes, not implementation. A test earns its keep by failing when
behaviour breaks: one that mirrors the implementation only makes refactoring expensive,
and one that cannot fail is dead weight. Cover the seams and the edges that actually
bite. Coverage percentage is not the goal, and deleting a test that no longer
distinguishes anything is a real improvement.

## Fail loudly

No code path continues past a condition it did not plan for. Four bans, and both halves
are in scope:

- No `except Exception`, and no `catch` that continues.
- No default standing in for a failure — no `or {}`, no `?? []`, no quietly returned
  `None` or `undefined`.
- No failure signalled by a return value a caller can drop. If continuing would be
  wrong, raise or throw.
- No warning where the code cannot correctly proceed.

Not crashing is legitimate only when all three of these hold: the design plans for the
condition, the contract names it, and the code reports it. Fewer than three, and it
raises.

This is not a style preference, because in an app with two halves **a silent failure
looks like an empty screen**. A route that swallows a store error answers `200 []` and
the table renders "No tasks yet". A `catch` around a mutation leaves the optimistic
update on screen and the write on the floor. A mock handler that answers a case the
backend refuses makes the contract suite green against a shape the real service never
returns. Every one of those reads as working software — and a test that asserts the
*absence* of an effect passes just as happily against the bug. Assert the failure
itself.

The contract is where this is cheapest to get right: declare every status code a route
can return, with a model for anything that has a body, and the other half then cannot
fail to handle it without a type error. See *Declare responses fully* below.

## Zero comments

No explanatory comments, no docblocks, no TODO/FIXME notes, no lint or type suppression
directives (`biome-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, `@ts-ignore`, `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`),
no commented-out code. Shebangs and TypeScript `///` directives are executable
directives, not comments.

Express intent through names, structure, types, and tests. Rationale goes in the commit
message; a decision goes in `docs/adr/`. This relocates rationale rather than removing it,
so a repo that adopts the rule and still writes `fix: bug` has simply deleted the
explanation.

**An ADR cites nothing by section number.** Name the thing — the route, the function,
the rule, the invariant — and let the reader grep. A section number points into one
revision of one document, and an ADR outlives the document whose structure it borrowed.

One carve-out: a published public API. Where a package is consumed outside this repo its
exported surface carries JSDoc or a docstring, because that text is shipped documentation,
not an explanation aimed at a reader of the source. Everything internal stays bare. Such a
doc states contracts, not reasoning: behaviour, failure, timing, ownership, safe use. Link
the rationale; don't restate it.

Scope: source, tests, scripts. Config files may carry comments where the format offers no
other way to explain a rule, and that includes `frontend/*.config.ts`, which is
configuration that happens to be written in TypeScript. **Vendored and generated code is
out of scope entirely** — see below.

**The rule is enforced, because on its own it does not hold.** `make lint` fails on any
comment token under `frontend/{src,tests,e2e,devtools}` and `backend/{app,tests,devtools}`
— `frontend/devtools/comments.mjs` and `backend/devtools/comments.py` are the two gates.
An agent reads this file at the top of a session and explains in place anyway, because
that is what the training data does. The suppression half is the half most worth
mechanising: refusing the spelling turns a threshold decision taken silently at the point
of pain into either a fix in the code or a reviewable line in `biome.json`,
`tsconfig.json` or `pyproject.toml`.

The guardrails this project ships are described in
[docs/agent-tooling.md](docs/agent-tooling.md), including where each threshold came from,
why the conformance checks fail closed, and why the agent guard fails open.

## The two halves

This repository is one application in two halves:

- **`frontend/`** — a React app. Everything pnpm touches lives here.
- **`backend/`** — a FastAPI service. Everything uv touches lives here.

Each half installs, lints, tests and builds on its own, without the other's toolchain
present. Run `pnpm` from `frontend/` and `uv` from `backend/`, or use the root `make`
targets, which fan out to both. There is no root `package.json`, and `pnpm install` at the
root will fail — that is deliberate.

## The contract

`openapi.json` at the repository root is what the halves agree on, and it is derived from
the backend, never written by hand:

```
backend/app/{models,routes}.py  ->  openapi.json  ->  frontend/src/api/schema.ts
                                                       -> types.ts, mocks/handlers.ts
```

After any change to a pydantic model or a route, run **`make openapi`** and commit
`openapi.json` and `frontend/src/api/schema.ts` alongside the source change. CI fails
otherwise, and so does `make openapi-check`.

Both artifacts are committed so each half stays independently operable — the frontend
regenerates types, tests and builds with no Python installed at all.

Three things about the spec are deliberate and should not be "fixed":

- **`422` responses appear on every route with a parameter or a body.** FastAPI adds them,
  along with `HTTPValidationError` and `ValidationError` schemas. They describe real
  behaviour.
- **Every error response declares a `model`.** A `404` declared without one says the
  response has no body, while `HTTPException` returns `{"detail": ...}` — the spec would be
  lying, and the typed handlers would refuse to mock it.
- **`redirect_slashes` is off.** With it on, `/api/tasks/` answers with a redirect to the
  backend's own origin, which escapes the dev proxy and fails CORS in a browser.

If the two artifacts conflict during a merge, do not hand-merge them. Take either side and
run `make openapi`.

**The mock handlers and the backend are two implementations of one contract.**
`frontend/tests/contract.test.ts` is run against both — `pnpm test` uses the handlers,
`make test-contract` starts both halves and runs the same file against the real service
through the dev proxy. Assertions there describe the contract only: shapes and status
codes, never seed data, which the two are free to differ on.

## Vendored and generated code — do not tidy

Two kinds of file here were written by a tool, and neither is yours to style.

**Vendored** — a tool wrote it, this project owns it, and you may change how it *behaves*:

- `frontend/src/components/ui/**` and `frontend/src/lib/utils.ts` —
  [shadcn](https://ui.shadcn.com/) components, added with `pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add
  <component>`. shadcn copies source into the repo; that source is yours.
- `frontend/public/mockServiceWorker.js` — written by Mock Service Worker's installer.
  Never hand-edit it; regenerate with `pnpm dlx msw init public --save`.

**Generated** — regenerate-only, and a hand edit is a bug:

- `openapi.json` and `frontend/src/api/schema.ts` — see *The contract*. Change the backend
  and run `make openapi`.

Both kinds are excluded from lint, from the complexity ratchet, and from the conformance
checks. Do not reformat them, strip their comments, or "simplify" them to match house
style.

## Build and Test

`make` at the root is the interface across both halves:

```bash
make install        # both halves, then activate the versioned git hooks
make pre-commit     # the gate: lint-check + openapi-check + test
make dev            # both halves: backend on :8000, frontend on :5173 in live mode
make dev-frontend   # frontend only, mock mode -- no backend required
make dev-backend    # backend only, with reload
make lint           # auto-fix and lint both halves
make lint-check     # check-only variant
make test           # both halves, then the contract suite against the real backend
make test-fast      # both halves only
make test-contract  # the contract suite against the backend half
make test-e2e       # opt-in: Playwright in mock mode (installs chromium)
make test-e2e-live  # opt-in: the same against a running `make dev`
make db-test        # opt-in: the store suites against a real Postgres it starts itself
make db             # a Postgres to develop against; prints the DATABASE_URL to export
make schema         # regenerate deploy/schema.sql after a migration entry
make openapi        # regenerate the contract artifacts
make openapi-check  # fail if the committed artifacts are stale
make build          # production build of the frontend
make upgrade        # update both halves' dependencies, then regenerate the contract
```

Or work in one half with its own tools: `pnpm -C frontend test:watch`,
`cd backend && uv run pytest -q tests/test_tasks.py`, `pnpm -C frontend add some-package`,
`cd backend && uv add some-package`.

**The checks live before the commit, not in CI.** `make pre-commit` is the gate, the git
hook `make hooks` installs runs it for you, and CI runs the same list. **New checks go in
`make pre-commit`** — a check there costs seconds, and the same check discovered on
GitHub costs a billed minute plus the round trip to find out which of your last four
commits broke it. `backend/tests/test_gate.py` fails when the gate, the hook and the
workflow stop agreeing, so the three cannot drift apart quietly.

The hook skips a half this clone has not installed, because working in one half alone is
legitimate here. That is the one dangerous thing about it — a run where everything
skipped exits 0 and looks exactly like a run where everything passed — so a partial run
says on stderr what it did not check. Read that before believing the green.

**Three things are deliberately not in the gate**, and all three need something fetched or
started first. `make test-e2e` and `make test-e2e-live` need a Playwright binary, and the
live one needs `make dev` in another terminal. `make db-test` needs a Docker daemon. A gate
that fetches any of those is a gate people learn to commit around, and `test_gate.py`
asserts all three stay out of it. Opt-in is not the same as deleted — see *See what you
built*, and run `make db-test` after touching `app/store/`.

## See what you built

You have a browser. Use it. After a UI change, run the app and look at it rather than
inferring from the JSX:

```bash
make test-e2e       # the mock-mode specs in e2e/; installs chromium the first time
```

To check the real system end to end, run `make dev` in one terminal and then:

```bash
make test-e2e-live
```

To look at a screen the specs don't cover, add a throwaway spec in `frontend/e2e/` that
navigates to it and calls `await page.screenshot({ path: "shot.png" })`, run it, and read
the image. Delete the spec afterwards unless it earned its keep as a test. A screenshot is
evidence; reasoning about markup is a guess.

These specs run in neither the gate nor CI, which makes them the one tier nothing will
tell you that you skipped. `frontend/e2e/tasks.live.spec.ts` is the live one and it will
fail against a backend that is not up; that failure is the point, and it is not a reason
to delete the spec.

## Frontend conventions

- **Layout**: `src/api/` is the contract with the backend (generated types plus a typed
  `fetch` wrapper), `src/mocks/` is the fake implementation of that contract,
  `src/components/` is UI, `src/router.tsx` wires routes. Component tests live in `tests/`,
  end-to-end specs in `e2e/`.

- **The mock layer is permanent.** `src/mocks/handlers.ts` is both a zero-backend
  development mode and the fixture layer every component test runs against. It is typed
  against the spec by [openapi-msw](https://github.com/christoph-fricke/openapi-msw), so a
  handler that returns an undeclared status code or the wrong shape is a type error rather
  than a surprise at runtime. Do not delete `src/mocks/` when the backend feels real
  enough — you would delete the test suite's foundation with it.

- **Modes**: `.env.development` and `.env.mock` set `VITE_ENABLE_MSW=true`; `.env.live`
  turns it off so requests reach the backend through the `/api` proxy in
  `vite.config.ts`. All three are committed and hold no secrets. The agent guard blocks
  writes to any `.env*` file, which is the right default and applies here too — if one
  needs to change, say so and let a human edit it.

- **Never assume the app is served from `/`.** The service worker's registration URL, the
  API base in `src/api/base.ts`, and the router's basepath all derive from
  `import.meta.env.BASE_URL`. A hard-coded leading slash works on your laptop and breaks
  under a subpath, including the worker's scope, which silently stops it intercepting
  anything. The Playwright suite runs against a subpath build to catch exactly this.

- **Imports**: use the `@/` alias for anything under `src/`.

- **TypeScript**: `strict`, plus `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`.
  Request and response types come from `src/api/types.ts`, which aliases the generated
  schema — change the backend, not those aliases.

- **Styling**: [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/) v4, configured in CSS. There is no
  `tailwind.config.js` — the theme is the `@theme` block in `src/index.css`, and it is the
  only place a colour or a type size is defined. Reach for design tokens
  (`bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`) over raw colours, and for the type and
  spacing scales (`text-sm`, `p-4`, `gap-6`) over arbitrary values. This is enforced:
  `pnpm conformance` fails on a hex literal, a colour keyword, a fixed palette step like
  `bg-blue-500`, a `text-[13px]`, and a `p-[7px]`. The reason is dark mode and drift — a
  literal renders perfectly in the theme you are looking at and keeps its value in the one
  you are not. Add a token to `src/index.css` instead.

  Two arbitrary values stay legal, because neither is an escape from the theme: sizing
  (`w-[240px]`, `max-w-[65ch]` — there is no token for how wide a sidebar is) and any
  value that reads a theme variable (`gap-[--spacing(var(--gap))]`).

- **Data**: server state comes from TanStack Query hooks against `src/api/client.ts`.
  Never fetch in a `useEffect`: it skips the cache, the deduplication, and the loading and
  error states the rest of the UI is written against. Effects are for synchronising with
  something outside React — a subscription, an event listener, imperative DOM work — and
  those are fine. Derived values are computed during render, not stored in state and
  refreshed by an effect.

- **Components**: prefer composing what is already in `src/components/ui/`. To add
  another, run `pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add <name>` rather than writing it by hand.

- **Lint/format**: [biome](https://biomejs.dev/) handles both, with its React domain rules
  on. Type checking is `tsc --noEmit`.

## Backend conventions

- **Layout**: `app/` is the service — `main.py` builds the FastAPI app, `models.py` holds
  the pydantic models the contract is made of, `routes.py` declares the endpoints,
  `deps.py` is what a route is handed, `wiring.py` chooses the substrate, and `store/` is
  the data layer. Tests live in `tests/`.

- **It is an application, not a library.** There is no build backend, no version, and
  nothing is published, so `[tool.uv] package = false` and `app` is importable from the
  project root rather than from site-packages. That is why `pytest` sets `pythonpath` and
  `devtools/export_openapi.py` adjusts `sys.path`.

- **The store is two implementations of one contract, and both are permanent.**
  `MemoryDatabase` is the default and `PostgresDatabase` takes over when `DATABASE_URL` is
  set. `tests/test_store_contract.py` runs one suite against both, which is what makes
  `TaskStore` a contract rather than a shape nothing checks — and it is the same pattern
  this repository already runs one layer up, where `frontend/tests/contract.test.ts` runs
  against the mock handlers and against this service. Do not delete the in-memory substrate
  when Postgres feels real enough: it is what `make test` and `make pre-commit` stand on,
  and the gate must not need a daemon. See
  [docs/adr/0001-two-substrates-behind-one-contract.md](docs/adr/0001-two-substrates-behind-one-contract.md).

- **Seed rows belong to one suite.** The in-memory substrate seeds three tasks and Postgres
  starts empty, deliberately: a suite that passes against both cannot have assumed either.
  `tests/test_tasks.py` is the only file allowed to know about the seeds, and ids are the
  same trap — memory hands out `"1"`, Postgres hands out uuids, and nothing may pattern-match
  one.

- **`wiring.build()` is the only reader of the environment.** Everything below it takes its
  DSN, schema and migrate mode as arguments. That is what lets one process hold two
  substrates, and why no test has to mutate `os.environ` to pick one. Nothing degrades from
  Postgres to memory on an error — a deployment that silently came up on memory has data
  that will not be there tomorrow, so a bad `DATABASE_URL` fails at boot.

- **The schema is data, and `deploy/schema.sql` is generated from it.** `app/store/ddl.py`
  holds one statement per entry under a four-digit key; run **`make schema`** and commit the
  result after adding one, the same way `openapi.json` follows the models.
  `tests/test_schema.py` fails when they drift. Two rules there are load-bearing and are
  explained in that file: never edit a shipped entry, and a column added later lands **twice**
  — in its table's `CREATE`, and as an `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` in the `0200_`
  repair band, which sorts above everything because `migrate()` compares `max(key)`.

- **The Postgres tier is opt-in.** `make db-test` starts its own `postgres:17-alpine` on an
  ephemeral port and tears it down; set `TEST_DATABASE_URL` to use your own instead. It is
  not in `make pre-commit` and not in CI, for the same reason the Playwright specs are not.
  `make db` gives you a database to develop against and prints the `DATABASE_URL` to export.

- **Declare responses fully.** Every status code a route can return belongs in the
  decorator, with a `model` for anything that has a body. The spec is generated from those
  declarations, and the frontend's handlers are typed from the spec, so an undeclared
  status code is invisible to the other half.

- **Lint/format**: [ruff](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/) for both, `basedpyright` for types,
  `codespell` for prose. `uv run python devtools/lint.py` runs the lot, which is what
  `make lint` calls.

## Both halves

- **Complexity**: each half gates its own, in its own unit, and the numbers do not
  transfer. The frontend gates per-function cognitive complexity, function length and file
  length, then checks codebase-wide density against `frontend/.complexity-baseline.json`.
  The backend gates cyclomatic complexity and statements per function, then checks its own
  density baseline. When any of them fails, split the function; raising a threshold or
  re-recording a baseline is a deliberate, reviewable decision, not the quick way to green.

- **Dependencies**: `pnpm add` in `frontend/`, `uv add` in `backend/`. Commit both
  lockfiles. A 14-day supply-chain cool-off applies on both sides, so a package published
  in the last fortnight will not resolve — including via `pnpm dlx shadcn add`. See
  [docs/development.md](docs/development.md), which also explains where the two halves'
  protections differ.

- **Agent session tracking**: this project ships [Entire](https://entire.io) hooks in
  `.claude/settings.json` that checkpoint agent coding sessions alongside git history.
  They no-op unless the `entire` CLI is on `PATH`; install it and run `entire enable` to
  activate, or delete `.claude/` and `.entire/` to opt out.

- **Agent guardrails**: `.claude/hooks/agent_guard.py` is a small, dependency-free
  `PreToolUse` hook that blocks a few unambiguously dangerous actions (`rm -rf /`, disk
  wipes, force-push to `main`/`master`, writing `.env` files or hard-coded secrets). It's
  a seatbelt, not a scanner, and fails open. Edit the patterns in that file, or remove its
  `PreToolUse` entry in `.claude/settings.json`, to change or drop it.

